cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use pixel-stream in academic or engineering work, please cite it as below."
title: "pixel-stream: a Lean-specified frame protocol for Godot pixel streaming"
abstract: >-
  A wire contract and a Lean 4 specification for streaming a Godot render over
  WebTransport. The Lean model proves monotone emit, no partial presentation, a
  bounded reassembly buffer, all-or-nothing frame admission and echo
  monotonicity; a generated corpus of delivery traces binds the header-only C++
  reassembler to that model. Ships no service. Bounded end-to-end latency is
  deliberately not proved -- there is no clock or network in the model -- and is
  measured instead.
type: software
license: Apache-2.0 OR MIT
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-09-20"
repository-code: "https://github.com/V-Sekai-fire/contract-pixel-stream"
keywords:
  - pixel-streaming
  - webtransport
  - quic
  - lean4
  - formal-specification
  - godot
references:
  - type: software
    title: "contract-http3-queue"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    abstract: >-
      Lean4 and Plausible model of the WebTransport/HTTP3 transport concurrency:
      a mutex-guarded inbound queue proven size-honest and a starvation-free
      network-thread loop. WHAT IS TAKEN is the layer boundary and the toolchain
      pin -- http3-queue owns inbound delivery, this repository owns outbound
      admission and frame reassembly. No proof or definition is copied, and the
      mutex is deliberately not re-modelled here.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/V-Sekai-fire/contract-http3-queue"
    license: MIT
  - type: software
    title: "Plausible"
    abstract: >-
      Lean-community property-testing library. Used as a Lake dependency for
      randomized cross-checks of the reassembly model. The build-time agreement
      between the model and its declared expectations is decided by
      `native_decide` rather than by Plausible, so a Plausible regression cannot
      silently weaken the corpus.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/leanprover-community/plausible"
    license: Apache-2.0
  - type: software
    title: "picoquic"
    authors:
      - name: "Private Octopus, Inc."
    abstract: >-
      Minimal QUIC implementation in C, and the backend behind
      `modules/http3/quic_picoquic_backend.cpp` in the entities-godot fork. This
      repository takes NO picoquic code. What it takes is the datagram size
      budget: the 1200-byte fragment in `wire.hpp` is chosen to fit a QUIC
      datagram inside the common 1280-byte IPv6 minimum MTU.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/private-octopus/picoquic"
    license: MIT
  - type: software
    title: "picotls"
    abstract: >-
      TLS 1.3 implementation that picoquic binds to for QUIC crypto, vendored in
      the engine fork at `thirdparty/picotls` and requiring `builtin_mbedtls`.
      Named here because it is in the trust path of any stream this protocol
      carries; no code is taken.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/h2o/picotls"
    license: MIT
  - type: software
    title: "GoPro CineForm SDK"
    authors:
      - name: "GoPro, Inc."
    abstract: >-
      Intra-frame wavelet codec, the workspace's sanctioned substitute for the
      blocklisted FFmpeg. Named as the incumbent the codec question is measured
      against, not as a dependency: this repository contains no codec code and
      the protocol is GOP-parameterized so an inter-frame codec needs no wire
      change. Its measured 0.47 Gbit/s at 1080p60 is the reason the codec
      question is open at all.
    repository-code: "https://github.com/gopro/cineform-sdk"
    license: Apache-2.0 OR MIT
  - type: standard
    title: "An Unreliable Datagram Extension to QUIC"
    authors:
      - name: "T. Pauly, E. Kinnear, D. Schinazi"
    year: 2022
    url: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9221.html"
    license: "IETF Trust License"
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "RFC 9221"
        description: "The datagram extension the unreliable path depends on."
    notes: >-
      The extension that makes fire-and-forget frame delivery possible without
      head-of-line blocking. This repository does not assume it is the right
      choice: one reliable stream per frame is a live alternative, and the README
      records why.
